          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %em exceptional field theory
            has come to be used for formulations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            which make the (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+Lie+group"}> exceptional
            , whence the name)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/U-duality"} U-duality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+group"} symmetry group
            structure manifest. This is in generalization of the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+field+theory"}> double field theory
            ” formulation of 10d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+supergravity"} type II supergravity
            which makes (only) the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-duality
            symmetry manifest.
          %p
            Accordingly, exceptional field theory is related to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+generalized+geometry"} exceptional generalized geometry
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+field+theory"} double field theory
            is related to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+complex+geometry"} generalized complex geometry
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            in exceptional field theory is locally modeled on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{1,d-1} \times R
          %p
            of a
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+space"} Minkowski space
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+representation"} fundamental representation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            of a form of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+Lie+group"} exceptional Lie group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mn 11
                      %mo −
                      %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_{11-d}
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/U-duality"} U-duality
            group). In the literature the former is called
            %em external space
            and the latter
            = succeed "." do
              %em internal space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equation"} differential equation
            derived from an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant"} invariant
            form of the representation and one considers a generalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry"} isometry
            algebra on this space which fails the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacobi+identity"} Jacobi identity
            by a term proportional to this contraint (e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BaguetHohmSamtleben15"} Baguet-Hohm-Samtleben 15, section 2
